Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome (Male)
Complex Fatigue Entities
Non-bacterial neuro-inflammatory prostatitis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-008
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of extreme pelvic pain and urinary retention concerns.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe, escalating perineal pain and significant difficulty voiding. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out acute urinary retention, acute bacterial prostatitis, or testicular torsion, and to manage the pain crisis effectively before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: It feels like I am sitting on a golf ball of glass, and it hurts so much to pass urine.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a progressive severe flare of perineal pain over the past 3 days. Symptoms include deep perineal aching, irritative voiding symptoms (dysuria, frequency), and a distinct post-exertional flare following physical exercise yesterday. Denies urethral discharge or macroscopic hematuria. The pain has escalated tonight to the point of severe distress, preventing sleep.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but severely uncomfortable while sitting or standing still.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high anxiety regarding potential permanent urinary dysfunction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Anxious, distressed, unable to sit comfortably, preferring to stand and pace.
Physical Exam: Abdomen soft, non-tender. Bladder non-palpable; no dullness to percussion in the suprapubic region, excluding acute gross retention. Genitourinary exam reveals normal epididymis and testes without swelling or tenderness. Digital rectal examination reveals a normal-sized, boggy, but exquisitely tender prostate without discrete nodules. Clinical presentation represents non-bacterial neuro-inflammatory prostatitis.
Environmental Observations: Clean residence, no immediate safety hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking Panadol osteo without relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Pelvic Pain Syndrome (Male) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute bacterial prostatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, rigors, or systemic toxicity.

Acute urinary retention - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient still able to void small amounts, and bladder is not palpable or percussible.

Testicular torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal, non-tender testicular examination and gradual onset of perineal pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's severe perineal pain and reported irritative voiding symptoms raised immediate red flags for acute urinary retention and acute bacterial prostatitis. A prompt after-hours home assessment was clinically imperative to manually palpate the bladder and exclude a urological emergency requiring catheterization. After ruling out bacterial infection and structural obstruction, targeted analgesic and anti-inflammatory therapy was required to break the neuro-inflammatory pain cycle before standard hours.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Meloxicam 15mg daily and a short course of Diazepam 5mg at night for pelvic floor muscle relaxation. Targeted intervention strategy includes PEA, Low Dose Tadalafil, and Amitriptyline. Urinalysis dipstick performed on-site (negative for nitrites/leukocytes).
Positive Behavioral Change: By utilizing a sitz bath or heating pad on the perineum for 20 minutes daily, you will actively relax the spastic pelvic floor muscles, directly diminishing the pressure pain and rewarding yourself with easier voiding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to specialized pelvic floor physiotherapist recommended.
DME/Safety (Optional): Donut cushion for sitting.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to discuss targeted neuro-inflammatory therapies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department if completely unable to pass urine for over 6 hours, or if high fever and rigors develop.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes negative mid-stream urine culture, which must be confirmed.
Clinical Course: Non-bacterial flares are extremely painful but not dangerous. Symptoms will slowly decrease over 1-2 weeks with pelvic floor relaxation and anti-inflammatory use.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest hospital immediately if your bladder becomes completely blocked and you cannot urinate at all.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain is driven by severe muscle spasm and nerve inflammation in the pelvic floor; the prescribed medications relax these muscles and reduce the nerve irritation.
Actionable Steps: Avoid strenuous core or lower body exercise until the flare resolves; stopping the physical trigger breaks the pain cycle, allowing the neuro-inflammatory state to calm down.

